  "why": "The only post-snapshot event that moves the problem is Montgomery–Sauermann. It improves the conjecture's own count of disjoint transversal bases to (1−o(1))n for large n, which is a bound and not an exact case, and only Kalai's commentary engages with it, so it stays unreviewed. Arndt et al. make a known asymptotic result constructive and prove no instance of the conjecture: none. Bérczi's AlphaEvolve runs certify sampled F₂-linear instances, which is a finite check: instances. Its AI role is generated, because the evolved policy produced the certificates, but the instances scope discards the event anyway. Divoux–Lowen–Wang is about a different Rota conjecture, a namesake: variant. Before the snapshot, the restricted classes and fixed ranks (n=3, n=4, paving, strongly base-orderable, graphic stars, char-0 vector spaces with n=p±1) are cases, and none is whole. Friedman–McGuinness and McGuinness 2021 give n−o(n) or n−k³ counts under hypotheses; I scored them as bound rather than case because they move the count without settling the exact statement.",
